# Copy to .env in this folder. Restart Expo after changes (use --clear once).
#
# Hosted server (recommended): same origin as your deployed Next app (https, no trailing slash).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://your-app.onrender.com
#
# Local dev only: same Wi‑Fi as the phone, your PC's LAN IP — not localhost from the phone.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.10:3000

# Google sign-in — use the same Web client ID string as the Next.js server (Google Cloud → Credentials).
# Either name works in Mobile/.env (app.config reads both). Restart Expo after changes (use --clear once).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=123456789-xxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=123456789-xxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
#
# Google Cloud → Web OAuth client → Authorized redirect URIs — add this https URL only (Google rejects custom schemes on Web clients):
#   https://auth.expo.io/@anonymous/vaultly
# Google redirect for EAS builds: use the URL from expo.dev when you open this project (e.g. https://auth.expo.io/@northino/vaultly). The app prefers embedded originalFullName over EXPO_PUBLIC_EXPO_AUTH_PROXY_PATH.
# Wrong @account (vs EAS project) causes Google to succeed then auth.expo.io shows "Something went wrong".
# EXPO_PUBLIC_EXPO_AUTH_PROXY_PATH=@anonymous/vaultly
#
# The Vaultly server must expose NEXTAUTH_SECRET (same as web) so POST /api/mobile/google can mint the app token — without it, mobile Google is blocked before the browser opens.
# On the server, GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID (or NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / AUTH_GOOGLE_ID) must match this Web client ID so GET /api/auth/social-config returns webClientId and googleMobile: true.
